"""
iptables manipulation helpers

these helpers are not safe for user input.



Some random notes about iptables:

On tables:
iptables rules are split into tables (filter, nat,...).
Every iptables command operates on a table,  specified with -t <table>
if -t is not specified, the default table is filter.
with iptables, available tables are hardcoded by the kernel:
    filter
    nat
    mangle
    raw
    security (SELinux)

portforward operates on filter and nat tables.

On chains:
inside a table, rules are sorted into chains.
inside a chain, rules are executed procedurally.
while rules can be put into builtin chains such as FORWARD,
but using dedicated chains helps manageing rules without breaking things.
The builtin chains and custom ones behave differently.

Builtin chains:
builtin chains have a policy for packets that match no rule. By default, this policy is always ACCEPT, but it can be changed by software (ufw sets the filter policy to DROP for input and forward trafic).
The builtin chains are:
    in the filter table:
        INPUT
        FORWARD
        OUTPUT
    in the nat table
        PREROUTING
        INPUT
        OUTPUT
        POSTROUTING
    mangle:
        PREROUTING
        INPUT
        FORWARD
        OUTPUT
        POSTROUTING
    raw:
        PREROUTING
        OUTPUT
    security:
        INPUT
        FORWARD
        OUTPUT
Policies can be:
    ACCEPT
    DROP
    REJECT (same as drop, but sends an error response with ICMP or TCP reset)
    (More detailed behaviour is possible within rules, where the "target" is the action done on the packet)

The policy of a builtin chain can be set with iptables -P <chain> <policy>


Custom chains:
Custom chains can be hooked to other chains so that their rules are executed as part of the parent chain, with a jump.
For example: iptables -t filter -A INPUT -j on-input-rules
Rules in custom chains are executed in order, and can RETURN to the parent chainto interrupt execution
Example: iptables -A on-input-rules -j RETURN
Chain jumps are actually just rules with a chain as their target, so it is possible to make conditional logic by adding filters on the jump rules
Example: iptables -A INPUT -p tcp -dport 22 -j ssh-chain
Though the applications seem limited to packet-level logic (i can't see any use for loops, for example)

For higher level stateful packet processing, see NFQUEUE (allows sending the packet to a userspace program to decide what to do with it).


On rules:
A rule must always have a target to jump to (-j).
Without explicit filters, a rule matches everything going through it and always executes.
Iptables accepts a rule description after these commands:
    -A append (most common, appends a rule at the end of a chain)
    -I insert (-I <chain> [<position>])
    -R replace
    -D delete
Basic rule definition examples:
    iptables -A my-chain -j ACCEPT
    which means: accept all packets sent to my-chain in the filter table
    iptables -A my-chain -j RETURN
    which means: after doing everything in my-chain, return
        note: this is already the default behavior of iptables, so this rule is a no-op
For more advanced rules, most filtering features must be explicitely loaded with -m <module> before using it:
        -m tcp (tcp options: ports, flags)
        -m udp (udp options: ports)
        -m mac (mac address)
        -m state (connection state: NEW or ESTABLISHED)
        ...




Some examples of iptables commands:
chain management:
iptables -N portforward # create a portforward chain in the filter table
iptables -X portforward # delete it
iptables -t nat -N portforward # same, but in the nat table
iptables -X portforward -t nat

iptables -P INPUT DROP  # drop all incoming packets (set by ufw)
iptables -P FORWARD DROP  # drop all packets to route (set by ufw)

The policy of a builtin chain can be set with iptables -P <chain> <policy>

Rules:
iptables -A <chain> -j <target> # adding a rule
Jump rules:
iptables -A some-chain -j my-new-chain

Other actions can be considered as just jumps to builtin targets:
iptables -A my-chain -j ACCEPT  # accept packets sent through my-chain
iptables -A my-chain -j RETURN  # return from my-chain
iptables -I my-chain 3 -j RETURN  # insert an early return at position 3
    note: rules are indexed starting at 1
    (-I my-chain without an index is the same as -I my-chain 1)

Filters and target arguments are sent as cli parameters:
# the rule applies to packets with a destination port 22 that are marked with 1
# and rejects them with a tcp-reset answer
iptables -t mangle -A PREROUTING --dport 22 -m mark --mark 1 -j REJECT --reject-with tcp-reset

Replace:
iptables -R my-chain 2 -j DROP  # replace the 2nd rule in my-chain with a new drop rule

Deletion:
iptables -D my-chain 2  # delete the second rule
iptables -D my-chain -p tcp -j ACCEPT  # delete the rule by describing it exactly



"""


# helper for building iptables commands
def scope(name="filter"):
    if name is None:
        name = "filter"
    return f"-t {name}"

def chain_exists(name, table=None):
    cmd = f"iptables {scope(table)} -L {name}"
    #print(cmd)
    out, code = system(cmd)
    success = (code == 0)
    return success

def create_chain(name, table=None):
    #if not chain_exists(name, table):
    cmd = f"iptables {scope(table)} -N {name}"
    out, code = system(cmd)
    return (code == 0)

# will not delete the chain if there are rules or jumps using it
# (empty the chain first with empty_chain(), and remove the chain jumps)
def delete_chain(name, table=None):
    cmd = f"iptables {scope(table)} -X {name}"
    out, code = system(cmd)
    return (code == 0)

def empty_chain(name, table=None):
    cmd = f"iptables {scope(table)} -F {name}"
    #print(cmd)
    out, code = system(cmd)
    #print(out, code)
    return (code == 0)

